[Start "[0,0,0]"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 d5 4. Bg2 Bb4+ {Neiksans stresses that this check is not cosmetic: it provokes a committed reply and interferes with White's ideal Catalan development.} (4... dxc4 {The open Catalan is playable, but the lecture repeatedly warns Black not to worship the extra pawn. White's activity and the g2 bishop can become more important than material.} 5. Nf3 c5 6. O-O cxd4 7. Nxd4 Be7 8. Qa4+ Nbd7 9. Qxc4 O-O {If Black accepts early, develop quickly and be ready to return the pawn rather than drifting into passivity.}) 5. Bd2 (5. Nd2 {If White blocks with Nd2, Black can more comfortably use a Queen's Indian style plan.} O-O 6. Nf3 b6 7. O-O Bb7 8. a3 Be7 {Plan: ...c5, ...Nbd7, ...Rc8, and sometimes ...Re8. The knight on d2 makes White's fast e4 setup less natural.}) (5. Nc3 O-O 6. Nf3 dxc4 {Against Nc3/Nf3 setups the c4 pawn is a more serious target. Black can combine the capture with ...Nc6 and queenside expansion.} 7. O-O Nc6 8. a3 Be7 9. e4 a6 10. a4 Rb8) Be7 (5... Bd6!? {Sharper than ...Be7. Neiksans calls the bishop-d6 setup aggressive because Black eyes ...dxc4 and ...e5, and can even head for Stonewall-like ideas with ...Ne4 and ...f5 in some move orders.} 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O c6 8. Nc3 Nbd7 9. b3 dxc4 10. bxc4 e5 {The point: if White has not stabilized the centre, Black strikes with ...e5 and avoids being squeezed.} 11. e4 exd4 12. Nxd4 Re8) 6. Nf3 O-O (6... dxc4?! {After Black has provoked Bd2 and retreated, Neiksans says taking on c4 usually makes less sense: White has spent a useful tempo and Black can be left defending passively.} 7. O-O O-O 8. Qc2 a6 9. Qxc4 b5 10. Qc2 Bb7 11. Rc1 {White regains the pawn and keeps easy pressure.}) (6... b6?! 7. cxd5! {A key Catalan structural rule from the lecture: when Black plays ...b6 too early in this d5/e6 structure, White should often take on d5.} exd5 8. Nc3 O-O 9. Rc1 c6 {Black can still play ...c6, but now it is passive rather than a clean c-pawn recapture. White will pressure c7/c6 and may provoke hanging pawns after ...c5.} 10. Ne5 Bb7 11. Bf4) 7. O-O c6 {This is the important closed-Catalan detail. Include ...c6 before ...b6 so that cxd5 can be met by ...cxd5, leaving no c7 weakness behind.} 8. Qc2 (8. b3 Nbd7 9. Nc3 b6 10. Qc2 Bb7 11. Rad1 Rc8 {Black has the desired closed setup: the bishop can go to b7, the rook to c8, and the main freeing break is ...c5.}) Nbd7 9. Rd1 b6 10. Bf4 (10. b3 Bb7 11. Nc3 Rc8 12. Rac1 Re8 {A calm version of the same plan. Black should not rush ...c5 if White can answer d5 and create a passed pawn; prepare it with pieces first.}) Bb7 11. Nc3 Rc8 12. Ne5 Nh5 {A practical Black move from the existing weak-opening file: challenge Bf4 and reduce White's easy piece pressure.} (12... Nxe5 13. dxe5 Nd7 {Also playable: trade the active knight and then prepare ...Qc7 or ...Re8 while watching the c5 break.}) 13. Bc1 Nhf6 14. e4 Nxe4 15. Nxe4 dxe4 16. Bxe4 f5 {A representative way to stop White from enjoying a free central bind. Black must meet e4 with concrete play, not passive waiting.} *
